  "id": "ch4-g6",
  "title": "The ...g6 setups",
  "intro": "This is the system's least comfortable ground and the chapter says so honestly. Black plays ...g6 and ...Bg7, and the two ideas the Zukertort is built on both lose their point.\n\n## Why the usual plan misfires\n\nThe bishop on d3 aims at h7. A pawn on g6 defends h7, so the bishop is aiming at a square that is already covered, and it can be hit by ...Nh5 and ...Bf5 into the bargain. Put it on e2 instead, where it is modest and useful.\n\nThe knight on e5 wants to be a permanent guest. Against a bishop on g7 it is not: ...Nfd7 or ...Nc6 challenges it, and every trade on e5 helps Black, because the bishop on g7 is aiming down the same diagonal your knight is standing on.\n\n## What to play instead\n\nb3, Bb2, and then c4. Hold the centre with pawns on c4 and d4, castle, and play a normal, solid game in what is close to a Queen's Indian with colours reversed. The bishop on b2 is a good piece here even without an attack behind it, because it stares straight at the bishop on g7 and neutralises it.\n\nThat is the whole recipe: swap the attacking setup for a sound one, and take a small pull instead of a big one. The position is roughly level and it is easy to play, which for a club game is worth more than a plan that does not work.\n\n## Move order\n\nBe2 before b3. It costs nothing, it prepares castling, and it keeps the option of meeting an early ...c5 or ...d5 without having committed the queenside.\n\n## Typical mistakes at this level\n\nPlaying Bd3 out of habit against a fianchetto. Playing Ne5 and letting Black trade it off with tempo on the bishop. Pushing pawns on the kingside where Black is solid and you are not. And drifting: this chapter is about getting a sound game, not about winning in twenty moves.",
